00:01:33.396 --> 00:01:36.196
and deep on everything
regarding learning Science

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:39.326
and academics and the best
way that folks can learn

00:01:39.326 --> 00:01:41.046
and help others learn.

00:01:41.046 --> 00:01:44.066
So Kristen, what I want to understand

00:01:44.066 --> 00:01:45.676
is what tips do you have?

00:01:45.676 --> 00:01:47.116
The question I get a lot is

00:01:47.116 --> 00:01:49.426
how do we keep people motivated right now,

00:01:49.426 --> 00:01:51.166
how do we keep people engaged,

00:01:51.166 --> 00:01:54.226
how do we kids are a
lot more of independence

00:01:54.226 --> 00:01:55.116
as expected of them.

00:01:55.116 --> 00:01:58.316
So what tips do you have
for students and parents

00:01:58.316 --> 00:02:00.166
and teachers trying to navigate this?

00:02:01.326 --> 00:02:03.776
- Yes, that is the question all of us

00:02:03.776 --> 00:02:06.166
here at Khan Academy are getting a lot of,

00:02:06.166 --> 00:02:07.706
so it's clearly something

00:02:07.706 --> 00:02:10.596
that people are struggling
with quite a bit.

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:12.746
As you said, I like to
look to the research

00:02:12.746 --> 00:02:14.066
and see what it says.

00:02:14.066 --> 00:02:16.176
And when you look across things,

00:02:16.176 --> 00:02:19.376
we can actually think pretty simply about

00:02:19.376 --> 00:02:22.816
what keeps people motivated, two things.

00:02:22.816 --> 00:02:24.786
First, we're motivated to do things

00:02:24.786 --> 00:02:27.196
when we think we're gonna
feel successful at them

00:02:27.196 --> 00:02:28.286
which kind of makes sense.

00:02:28.286 --> 00:02:29.346
Like who wants to do something

00:02:29.346 --> 00:02:30.646
if you think you're gonna fail at it.

00:02:30.646 --> 00:02:32.506
Doesn't seem very fun.

00:02:32.506 --> 00:02:34.976
And then second, if we value the activity.

00:02:34.976 --> 00:02:37.066
If it's something that's important to us.

00:02:37.066 --> 00:02:40.436
So really we can boil there on a lot of

00:02:40.436 --> 00:02:42.216
the tips and ideas and things

00:02:42.216 --> 00:02:45.586
to things that are either going
to help us feel successful

00:02:45.586 --> 00:02:48.506
or increase our perception of

00:02:48.506 --> 00:02:50.346
the value of the activity we're doing

00:02:50.346 --> 00:02:52.296
and sometimes both of those things.

00:02:52.296 --> 00:02:55.236
So that's kind of the
simple way of the land.

00:02:55.236 --> 00:02:58.706
And so to your question
about a couple of tips.

00:02:58.706 --> 00:03:02.906
One really powerful thing
we can do, is to set goals.

00:03:02.906 --> 00:03:04.126
Because goal setting

00:03:04.126 --> 00:03:06.946
and then monitoring
ourselves, hits on both

00:03:06.946 --> 00:03:11.026
of those two things
about success and value.

00:03:11.026 --> 00:03:14.286
And so if I set a goal
or if you're a parent

00:03:14.286 --> 00:03:16.756
or a teacher and you work with

00:03:16.756 --> 00:03:18.416
the kid to set the goal,

00:03:18.416 --> 00:03:21.136
so not just impose it on them,

00:03:21.136 --> 00:03:22.816
but let them help you think about

00:03:22.816 --> 00:03:24.266
what should your goal be?

00:03:24.266 --> 00:03:27.656
That gives you some buy-in
it increases the value

00:03:27.656 --> 00:03:30.216
because you're thinking, yeah I'm gonna,

00:03:30.216 --> 00:03:31.406
this is something I care about.

00:03:31.406 --> 00:03:32.916
I'm gonna set the goal to be something

00:03:32.916 --> 00:03:34.996
that's important to me.

00:03:34.996 --> 00:03:38.706
And then, as you watch
yourself make progress

00:03:38.706 --> 00:03:40.966
toward that goal and actually reach it,

00:03:40.966 --> 00:03:43.106
it turns out you're experiencing success

00:03:43.106 --> 00:03:45.426
and there's nothing that convinces you

00:03:45.426 --> 00:03:46.866
you might be successful in the future.

00:03:46.866 --> 00:03:49.196
Like actually having some success.

00:03:49.196 --> 00:03:51.376
So these can be little goals,

00:03:51.376 --> 00:03:53.606
it can be I'm gonna write five sentences

00:03:53.606 --> 00:03:56.289
and then, hey, I wrote six, that's great.

00:03:57.516 --> 00:03:58.546
It can be I'm gonna do

00:03:58.546 --> 00:03:59.736
this many math problems.

00:03:59.736 --> 00:04:01.726
Start out small don't be like,

00:04:01.726 --> 00:04:06.446
I'm gonna learn all of algebra
in this next two months.

00:04:06.446 --> 00:04:08.296
That's too long, it's too much

00:04:08.296 --> 00:04:11.526
you're gonna not be able to sustain that.

00:04:11.526 --> 00:04:14.077
So keep it small, but
be able to think about,

00:04:14.077 --> 00:04:16.676
what some of those achievable things

00:04:16.676 --> 00:04:18.239
that you can do are.

00:04:19.876 --> 00:04:20.709
- I like that.

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:22.186
So the two big takeaways,

00:04:22.186 --> 00:04:25.006
you should have a reasonable chance of,

00:04:25.006 --> 00:04:26.866
or belief that you can be successful

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:29.286
or feeling a successful
and it should be fun.

00:04:29.286 --> 00:04:32.130
But let me ask a question
sometimes like I'm

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.336
a hobbyist guitarist for example.

00:04:34.336 --> 00:04:37.186
And it's very tempting for me to in

00:04:37.186 --> 00:04:39.296
the spirit of wanting to feel successful,

00:04:39.296 --> 00:04:41.286
kind of play the songs that I already know

00:04:41.286 --> 00:04:43.056
because I know I'll succeed at them

00:04:43.056 --> 00:04:48.056
and it's fun 'cause I can
kind of get into the more.

00:04:48.346 --> 00:04:49.186
Is that what you're saying?

00:04:49.186 --> 00:04:50.856
Like, should I not challenge myself?

00:04:50.856 --> 00:04:52.696
Should I not try the songs

00:04:52.696 --> 00:04:53.776
that I'm still fumbling through

00:04:53.776 --> 00:04:55.726
these bizarre chords and whatever else?

00:04:56.576 --> 00:04:59.276
- That's definitely not what we wanna say.

00:04:59.276 --> 00:05:01.046
In fact, we wanna think about

00:05:01.046 --> 00:05:04.606
how we can keep moving forward.

00:05:04.606 --> 00:05:06.986
And one of the ways
that we can frame that,

00:05:06.986 --> 00:05:10.106
is actually to think about
this idea of growth mindset

00:05:10.106 --> 00:05:12.056
which has gotten a lot of play lately

00:05:12.056 --> 00:05:14.346
and sometimes it feels
like, Oh, growth mindset

00:05:14.346 --> 00:05:16.096
is just saying, hey, work hard

00:05:16.096 --> 00:05:16.929
and you can do it.

00:05:16.929 --> 00:05:18.486
But when it comes to learning,

00:05:18.486 --> 00:05:21.986
what we know is that when
you're learning new things,

00:05:21.986 --> 00:05:25.376
you're actually changing
how your brain works.

00:05:25.376 --> 00:05:27.809
And we actually have a course,

00:05:30.686 --> 00:05:33.266
how to the courses section down there

00:05:33.266 --> 00:05:35.526
in the life skills.

00:05:35.526 --> 00:05:38.636
And it actually walks students through

00:05:38.636 --> 00:05:43.276
some of these ideas about how
our brain actually changes

00:05:43.276 --> 00:05:46.076
the neurons little cells in our brains

00:05:46.076 --> 00:05:47.296
when we're learning new things,

00:05:47.296 --> 00:05:49.376
make new connections.

00:05:49.376 --> 00:05:52.436
And we can we explain that in some videos

00:05:52.436 --> 00:05:55.046
and students can walk
through it a little bit.

00:05:55.046 --> 00:05:57.576
But this idea that your brain
is actually like a muscle.

00:05:57.576 --> 00:05:58.966
It's like when you're working out.

00:05:58.966 --> 00:06:01.856
And if you keep lifting heavier weights,

00:06:01.856 --> 00:06:03.786
your muscles get bigger in the same way

00:06:03.786 --> 00:06:05.904
that if you keep learning new things,

00:06:05.904 --> 00:06:08.236
your brain actually gets denser

00:06:08.236 --> 00:06:11.236
and it turns out dense
brains are a good thing.

00:06:11.236 --> 00:06:13.546
And you can learn new
things and build them.

00:06:13.546 --> 00:06:15.696
So if you keep playing
your same songs over

00:06:15.696 --> 00:06:17.716
and over again, you're not building any of

00:06:17.716 --> 00:06:22.046
these new muscle and this new
connections in your brain.

00:06:22.046 --> 00:06:23.946
But if you're trying new things,

00:06:23.946 --> 00:06:25.726
you're going to keep improving.

00:06:25.726 --> 00:06:27.556
And next time you'll be able to play

00:06:27.556 --> 00:06:29.716
even more complicated songs

00:06:29.716 --> 00:06:31.386
and maybe some other songs

00:06:31.386 --> 00:06:33.479
that you're interested in learning.

00:06:34.498 --> 00:06:36.596
- So if I were to kind of figure out

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:38.326
the sweet spot there you
definitely shouldn't work

00:06:38.326 --> 00:06:39.266
on the stuff that is,

00:06:39.266 --> 00:06:40.916
you're definitely going to succeed on

00:06:40.916 --> 00:06:42.156
because that's not going to stretch you

00:06:42.156 --> 00:06:43.546
and grow those neural connections.

00:06:43.546 --> 00:06:45.739
And maybe don't work on the thing that is,

00:06:47.076 --> 00:06:49.466
for me the Eddie van Halen level

00:06:49.466 --> 00:06:50.299
because I might not become...

00:06:50.299 --> 00:06:52.747
- I was gonna say what's
this, super hard guitar.

00:06:52.747 --> 00:06:55.146
(laughing)

00:06:55.146 --> 00:06:58.146
- I avoid that, but try the stuff

00:06:58.146 --> 00:06:59.936
that's stretching me a little bit.

00:06:59.936 --> 00:07:01.646
And another thing I took away from you

00:07:01.646 --> 00:07:02.926
is when someone calls me dance

00:07:02.926 --> 00:07:04.616
which has happened.

00:07:04.616 --> 00:07:06.555
I take that as a compliment from now on.

00:07:06.555 --> 00:07:07.388
- You should absolutely take

00:07:07.388 --> 00:07:08.316
that as a compliment.
- I'm sure they're referring

00:07:08.316 --> 00:07:10.926
to my neural density when
they say that I'm dance.

00:07:10.926 --> 00:07:12.636
- Exactly.
- So we have questions,

00:07:12.636 --> 00:07:15.016
I encourage everyone to ask questions.

00:07:15.016 --> 00:07:16.596
Any questions really about learning,

00:07:16.596 --> 00:07:19.256
about learning science
but education Khan Academy

00:07:19.256 --> 00:07:21.486
for Kristen and myself if relevant.

00:07:21.486 --> 00:07:24.636
We have a few questions
that are all coming in

00:07:24.636 --> 00:07:28.516
from Facebook, Cindy Victor Kumpen asks,

00:07:28.516 --> 00:07:30.174
my boy went to school for four years

00:07:30.174 --> 00:07:32.476
and it took away all of his motivation.

00:07:32.476 --> 00:07:33.996
It was boring every day.

00:07:33.996 --> 00:07:36.496
How can I help him getting
enthusiastic again,

00:07:36.496 --> 00:07:38.009
getting back on track?

00:07:38.855 --> 00:07:40.626
Your advice, Kristen?

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:41.909
- Well, first I'm sorry to hear that.

00:07:41.909 --> 00:07:44.556
That he's not still excited about learning

00:07:44.556 --> 00:07:45.816
and where those things are.

00:07:45.816 --> 00:07:47.846
So there's a couple
things that I would say.

00:07:47.846 --> 00:07:52.136
So one is, you know him
better than anyone else

00:07:52.136 --> 00:07:54.836
and you can help him make
the connections between

00:07:54.836 --> 00:07:57.076
what he's learning and the
things he's interested in.

00:07:57.076 --> 00:07:59.326
So when we talked about that value,

00:07:59.326 --> 00:08:01.956
one way that we value
things we're doing is

00:08:01.956 --> 00:08:04.146
because they seem relevant to us.

00:08:04.146 --> 00:08:06.846
So if you know the things
that he's learning,

00:08:06.846 --> 00:08:08.216
help him make those connections

00:08:08.216 --> 00:08:10.676
between why am I learning this

00:08:10.676 --> 00:08:13.496
and what it can do to relate to

00:08:13.496 --> 00:08:15.456
other things I'm interested in,

00:08:15.456 --> 00:08:17.716
questions he's asked about the world

00:08:17.716 --> 00:08:21.926
and how it works, things he
wants to be when he grows up.

00:08:21.926 --> 00:08:23.206
Any of those kinds of things,

00:08:23.206 --> 00:08:25.316
the way a way to help him see some of

00:08:25.316 --> 00:08:28.986
the meaning in the things
that he's being asked to do.

00:08:28.986 --> 00:08:32.406
So that's one way that
you can start bringing

00:08:32.406 --> 00:08:34.936
some of that piece back.

00:08:34.936 --> 00:08:37.096
And the other thing is
I would say at home,

00:08:37.096 --> 00:08:38.916
really encourage those questions

00:08:38.916 --> 00:08:43.186
and think about how
he's thinking about how

00:08:43.186 --> 00:08:46.216
the world works and wondering about this

00:08:46.216 --> 00:08:51.216
or the other thing and
when I peddle my bike,

00:08:51.236 --> 00:08:53.116
why does that make the wheel move

00:08:53.116 --> 00:08:56.326
and start even modeling, asking
those kinds of questions.

00:08:56.326 --> 00:08:57.366
And those are the things

00:08:57.366 --> 00:08:59.236
that then help you relate back to

00:08:59.236 --> 00:09:01.336
the things that the students are learning.

00:09:02.936 --> 00:09:04.986
- And I'll just add to that.

00:09:04.986 --> 00:09:07.616
I think there's two reasons
why I've seen either myself

00:09:07.616 --> 00:09:10.696
or other folks disengage
or feel something's boring.

00:09:10.696 --> 00:09:13.046
Oftentimes you feel that it's boring

00:09:13.046 --> 00:09:14.316
because you're ready to move ahead

00:09:14.316 --> 00:09:15.896
but you you're getting impatient

00:09:15.896 --> 00:09:17.606
or sometimes you feel it's boring

00:09:17.606 --> 00:09:19.246
that it's a little overwhelming,

00:09:19.246 --> 00:09:21.266
it's all going over
your head, so to speak.

00:09:21.266 --> 00:09:22.376
And so one option,

00:09:22.376 --> 00:09:25.076
and I know a good website for this

00:09:25.076 --> 00:09:26.409
or a good series of apps,

00:09:27.266 --> 00:09:29.246
especially in math and science,

00:09:29.246 --> 00:09:31.386
but we have some resources
and other things.

00:09:31.386 --> 00:09:34.856
But if your son I believe it was

00:09:34.856 --> 00:09:36.136
goes on to Khan Academy is able

00:09:36.136 --> 00:09:38.006
to put even 20, 30 minutes a day.

00:09:38.006 --> 00:09:39.436
Start at their level it sounds like

00:09:39.436 --> 00:09:41.936
your son's been going to
school for about four years,

00:09:41.936 --> 00:09:44.566
so maybe they're in fourth or fifth grade.

00:09:44.566 --> 00:09:46.226
Start them even at the kindergarten level

00:09:46.226 --> 00:09:48.076
or start them on the get
ready for fourth grade

00:09:48.076 --> 00:09:50.746
or get ready for fifth grade
course on Khan Academy.

00:09:50.746 --> 00:09:52.736
That will allow them to
fill in all their gaps,

00:09:52.736 --> 00:09:54.396
probably build a little bit of confidence.

00:09:54.396 --> 00:09:56.206
It will be a little bit of
playing those guitar songs

00:09:56.206 --> 00:09:57.426
that he already knows,

00:09:57.426 --> 00:09:59.296
but he might realize that a few

00:09:59.296 --> 00:10:00.926
of his chords need a
little bit more practice

00:10:00.926 --> 00:10:01.986
than he appreciates.

00:10:01.986 --> 00:10:03.436
And then when he gets to the grade level

00:10:03.436 --> 00:10:04.269
of course, he can learn about

00:10:04.269 --> 00:10:05.576
that in his own time and pace.

00:10:05.576 --> 00:10:08.326
And then he'll probably
not only get caught up

00:10:08.326 --> 00:10:10.426
with his class if he
wasn't already caught up,

00:10:10.426 --> 00:10:12.206
but probably get ahead of his class.

00:10:12.206 --> 00:10:13.216
And you know what I found

00:10:13.216 --> 00:10:14.846
and I'm even speaking
from personal experience.

00:10:14.846 --> 00:10:17.926
Once you see academic success in one area,

00:10:17.926 --> 00:10:20.876
you naturally start to get
more curious, more engaged,

00:10:20.876 --> 00:10:22.816
your perception of yourself starts to say,

00:10:22.816 --> 00:10:24.976
I'm a learner, I'm capable of learning

00:10:24.976 --> 00:10:26.426
and I don't have to wait for other people

00:10:26.426 --> 00:10:27.616
to tell me what to learn.

00:10:27.616 --> 00:10:29.086
I can do this on my own.

00:10:29.086 --> 00:10:31.186
And then that can bleed
over into other things

00:10:31.186 --> 00:10:34.156
that can bleed on to being
curious about science

00:10:34.156 --> 00:10:36.676
or humanities or politics
or whatever else.

00:10:36.676 --> 00:10:38.706
And a lot of what Kristen
just mentioned of,

00:10:38.706 --> 00:10:41.166
the real world, how does
it all fit together?

00:10:41.166 --> 00:10:46.166
I think you have a better shot of that.

00:10:46.326 --> 00:10:49.106
So let's see other questions here.

00:10:49.106 --> 00:10:52.226
So this from Greenwich
Connecticut, Kimberly's asking,

00:10:52.226 --> 00:10:54.046
how do I know if my child is learning

00:10:54.046 --> 00:10:55.846
if he isn't tested?

00:10:55.846 --> 00:10:58.192
Kimberly has children
in grades three, six,

00:10:58.192 --> 00:11:00.046
and eight, eighth grade.

00:11:00.046 --> 00:11:01.436
So how do you know if they're learning

00:11:01.436 --> 00:11:02.486
if they are not tested?

00:11:02.486 --> 00:11:04.886
Good question, Kristen your view.

00:11:04.886 --> 00:11:06.186
- That is a good question.

00:11:06.186 --> 00:11:09.726
So first, talk to your child's
teachers about what are

00:11:09.726 --> 00:11:12.546
the skills that they're
supposed to be learning.

00:11:12.546 --> 00:11:14.586
So not what score they get on a test,

00:11:14.586 --> 00:11:16.136
but what are the things

00:11:16.136 --> 00:11:17.756
that they should know how to do?

00:11:17.756 --> 00:11:18.976
What are the ideas

00:11:18.976 --> 00:11:22.346
that they should start
to be understanding?

00:11:22.346 --> 00:11:24.526
And then, there's a couple
of things you can do.

00:11:24.526 --> 00:11:27.006
So first is in that
conversation with the teacher,

00:11:27.006 --> 00:11:29.596
get their thoughts about, okay,

00:11:29.596 --> 00:11:31.921
where do you think my child is

00:11:31.921 --> 00:11:34.846
and getting to do those things.

00:11:34.846 --> 00:11:37.896
But you can also just
start observing the things

00:11:37.896 --> 00:11:39.909
and when you're at home and think about,

00:11:41.086 --> 00:11:42.686
asking them questions,

00:11:42.686 --> 00:11:44.576
having those conversations with them

00:11:44.576 --> 00:11:46.506
that gives you some evidence about, hey,

00:11:46.506 --> 00:11:49.463
how well did I think they
understand where this is

00:11:49.463 --> 00:11:51.416
and what this looks like?

00:11:51.416 --> 00:11:52.506
The other thing that I would say

00:11:52.506 --> 00:11:55.786
is I've done a lot of kind of thinking

00:11:55.786 --> 00:11:58.786
and writing about the idea
of assessment without tests.

00:11:58.786 --> 00:12:01.716
And the idea is that particularly now

00:12:01.716 --> 00:12:03.736
that we're in this digital world,

00:12:03.736 --> 00:12:06.656
we can get quite a bit of information

00:12:06.656 --> 00:12:09.416
as students are doing learning activities.

00:12:09.416 --> 00:12:11.656
Again, another shameless plug for

00:12:11.656 --> 00:12:14.946
the Khan Academy website
that helps students

00:12:14.946 --> 00:12:17.836
be able to see whether

00:12:17.836 --> 00:12:18.906
what kind of level they're at

00:12:18.906 --> 00:12:22.066
or have they mastered the skill or not.

00:12:22.066 --> 00:12:23.466
Without actually taking a test,

00:12:23.466 --> 00:12:26.566
they're getting kind of through
their practice activities.

00:12:26.566 --> 00:12:29.586
They are leveling up from
kind of being familiar to

00:12:29.586 --> 00:12:32.156
the task, to proficient with the task.

00:12:32.156 --> 00:12:34.986
And so by gathering that
data from digital activities,

00:12:34.986 --> 00:12:37.726
we actually can eliminate some of the need

00:12:37.726 --> 00:12:41.416
for the testing that's
kind of a big pressure

00:12:41.416 --> 00:12:43.966
on teachers time and on students time,

00:12:43.966 --> 00:12:46.646
it leads to anxiety and all
of those kinds of things.

00:12:46.646 --> 00:12:48.206
And instead of we can get information

00:12:48.206 --> 00:12:51.936
from them as they're learning,
to be able to understand.

00:12:53.986 --> 00:12:54.986
- Yeah, I'll just underline

00:12:54.986 --> 00:12:58.446
that last point of
practice on Khan Academy

00:12:58.446 --> 00:12:59.279
isn't just practice.

00:12:59.279 --> 00:13:01.806
It's inherently a little micro assessment

00:13:01.806 --> 00:13:03.006
every time a student engages.

00:13:03.006 --> 00:13:04.956
And so that is also a good way to read.

00:13:04.956 --> 00:13:07.426
And we've seen correlations,
very strong correlations

00:13:07.426 --> 00:13:09.066
between work on Khan Academy

00:13:09.066 --> 00:13:10.776
and other types of benchmark assessments.

00:13:10.776 --> 00:13:12.746
So that's, yeah completely agree.

00:13:12.746 --> 00:13:15.296
So from Facebook, there's a
question Jamie Dawson Osborne

00:13:15.296 --> 00:13:17.976
is asking what about when
students don't understand

00:13:17.976 --> 00:13:19.496
and the teacher's hands are tied

00:13:19.496 --> 00:13:21.566
with how they can teach the lesson.

00:13:21.566 --> 00:13:23.336
So how does either the student,

00:13:23.336 --> 00:13:25.786
the parent or the teacher
deal with that Kristen?

00:13:27.566 --> 00:13:30.800
- I'm not sure what hands are tied imply.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.289
(laughing)

00:13:32.289 --> 00:13:37.289
- My interpretation is the
teacher has to cover with

00:13:38.616 --> 00:13:40.106
the negative exponents

00:13:40.106 --> 00:13:42.026
and say a seventh grade math class.

00:13:42.026 --> 00:13:44.276
But the students still doesn't
understand basic exponents,

00:13:44.276 --> 00:13:46.346
but the teacher feels I have to cover

00:13:46.346 --> 00:13:47.776
this negative exponents standard

00:13:47.776 --> 00:13:50.316
because that's what my
pacing guide I got from

00:13:50.316 --> 00:13:51.676
the district tells me to do.

00:13:51.676 --> 00:13:54.926
How much of the various
parties do in that scenario,

00:13:54.926 --> 00:13:58.026
and you can imagine that's
very disengaging for students.

00:13:58.026 --> 00:13:59.606
- Absolutely.

00:13:59.606 --> 00:14:03.556
And I think what we
often see is this tension

00:14:03.556 --> 00:14:05.686
that you've talked about a lot

00:14:05.686 --> 00:14:08.896
between having to keep
marching forward according

00:14:08.896 --> 00:14:11.866
to what is in the scope and sequence,

00:14:11.866 --> 00:14:14.616
we call it, that list of
what needs to be taught

00:14:14.616 --> 00:14:18.146
when in the school year
versus making sure students

00:14:18.146 --> 00:14:20.646
have time to master what they're doing.

00:14:20.646 --> 00:14:23.536
And we really need the balance
of both of those things,

00:14:23.536 --> 00:14:24.806
because I'm looking at it is important

00:14:24.806 --> 00:14:27.076
that students have exposure to all of

00:14:27.076 --> 00:14:28.076
that grade level work.

00:14:28.076 --> 00:14:28.909
And on the other hand,

00:14:28.909 --> 00:14:31.806
it's also important that
they have the opportunity

00:14:31.806 --> 00:14:33.816
to fill in gaps of things that maybe

00:14:33.816 --> 00:14:35.616
they didn't quite master before

00:14:35.616 --> 00:14:37.996
or they have the time to do this.

00:14:37.996 --> 00:14:40.686
And so one of the things that we encourage

00:14:40.686 --> 00:14:44.596
is this idea of thinking
about potentially spending

00:14:44.596 --> 00:14:46.896
four days of the classroom per week on

00:14:46.896 --> 00:14:48.676
that grade level material.

00:14:48.676 --> 00:14:51.676
And then one day on what we
call Personalized Practice,

00:14:51.676 --> 00:14:55.906
the idea that you can
start working with students

00:14:55.906 --> 00:14:59.836
at the level where they are
thinking about filling in

00:14:59.836 --> 00:15:02.936
some of those gaps,
understanding where they are,

00:15:02.936 --> 00:15:05.706
what maybe didn't quite get to mastery on

00:15:05.706 --> 00:15:07.052
and giving them additional practice

00:15:07.052 --> 00:15:09.356
and instruction in those areas so that

00:15:09.356 --> 00:15:11.916
they can would then when they do again,

00:15:11.916 --> 00:15:14.056
get back to that grade level material,

00:15:14.056 --> 00:15:16.926
they're ready for it and able to do it.

00:15:16.926 --> 00:15:18.166
So I think that's one thing.

00:15:18.166 --> 00:15:20.561
The other thing that came to mind with

00:15:20.561 --> 00:15:24.326
the hands are tied piece is that sometimes

00:15:24.326 --> 00:15:25.656
the teachers explaining it

00:15:25.656 --> 00:15:28.156
and explaining it in the way
that they think about it,

00:15:28.156 --> 00:15:30.816
but just hearing someone
else explaining it

00:15:30.816 --> 00:15:34.356
can give you, Oh, aha, now I get it.

00:15:34.356 --> 00:15:37.506
And so that's another way
that some of our videos.

00:15:37.506 --> 00:15:39.126
I think it could be helpful is

00:15:39.126 --> 00:15:41.966
that they provide a little bit
different way to explain it

00:15:41.966 --> 00:15:42.996
or to think about it

00:15:42.996 --> 00:15:44.946
that someone hasn't maybe heard before.

00:15:46.669 --> 00:15:47.566
- Very, very helpful.

00:15:47.566 --> 00:15:48.702
Yeah, I completely agree.

00:15:48.702 --> 00:15:49.535
I think in that case,

00:15:49.535 --> 00:15:50.736
whatever we can do to help

00:15:50.736 --> 00:15:52.456
that student fill in their gaps

00:15:52.456 --> 00:15:55.226
so that they can engage,
it's going to be optimal.

00:15:55.226 --> 00:15:59.466
So a couple of related
questions Rifat Tanvir

00:15:59.466 --> 00:16:01.096
from Facebook is asking,

00:16:01.096 --> 00:16:03.280
do you think motivation is an illusion

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:05.456
they've been watching motivational videos

00:16:05.456 --> 00:16:07.776
but they feel like it hasn't
been helpful for them.

00:16:07.776 --> 00:16:11.656
A similar question from
YouTube, Miss Victoria,

00:16:11.656 --> 00:16:13.226
the eyes are all numbers there,

00:16:13.226 --> 00:16:15.826
but how can you become
more self-motivated?

00:16:15.826 --> 00:16:18.136
So yeah, you hear this
thing like motivation.

00:16:18.136 --> 00:16:19.416
Yes, so and so is motivated.

00:16:19.416 --> 00:16:21.076
So, and so is not motivated.

00:16:21.076 --> 00:16:21.909
What is that?

00:16:21.909 --> 00:16:24.676
Is that like some, do we
have like a motivation gene?

00:16:24.676 --> 00:16:27.176
Is it contextual?

00:16:27.176 --> 00:16:28.329
Can you improve it?

00:16:29.726 --> 00:16:33.186
- So absolutely it is not something

00:16:33.186 --> 00:16:34.836
that's preprogrammed in you

00:16:34.836 --> 00:16:36.419
and you either are or are not.

00:16:37.652 --> 00:16:40.786
It's very much contextual
you can be motivated

00:16:40.786 --> 00:16:43.436
to do one thing and not
motivated to do something else

00:16:43.436 --> 00:16:45.786
and that's very common.

00:16:45.786 --> 00:16:47.396
I hear lots of parents say, gosh

00:16:47.396 --> 00:16:51.956
my kid seems really
motivated to play Minecraft,

00:16:51.956 --> 00:16:54.666
but not motivated at all to read books.

00:16:54.666 --> 00:16:56.816
So it's definitely something

00:16:56.816 --> 00:17:00.256
that you can think about changing to

00:17:00.256 --> 00:17:01.678
the point of is it

00:17:01.678 --> 00:17:03.836
the thing that's not gonna change it,

00:17:03.836 --> 00:17:05.696
is like motivational slogans.

00:17:05.696 --> 00:17:10.236
So just, hey keep working,
hey you're doing great.

00:17:10.236 --> 00:17:12.306
Like, people don't really believe that

00:17:12.306 --> 00:17:14.576
if it's not their experience.

00:17:14.576 --> 00:17:17.136
And so there's definitely,

00:17:17.136 --> 00:17:18.536
we wanna think about that,

00:17:18.536 --> 00:17:20.526
this idea of authentic success

00:17:20.526 --> 00:17:23.636
and giving these messages
about why we think

00:17:23.636 --> 00:17:24.666
that they can do this

00:17:24.666 --> 00:17:26.356
and why we think they'll be successful

00:17:26.356 --> 00:17:27.252
which goes back to some of the things

00:17:27.252 --> 00:17:29.736
we were talking about earlier about,

00:17:29.736 --> 00:17:33.616
if you understand how this is
actually changing your brain

00:17:33.616 --> 00:17:35.726
and how working hard can actually lead

00:17:35.726 --> 00:17:38.376
to those kinds of
changes and improvements.

00:17:38.376 --> 00:17:41.096
That then can help think about, okay,

00:17:41.096 --> 00:17:43.836
now I understand why I might be motivated

00:17:43.836 --> 00:17:46.009
as opposed to just keep working hard.

00:17:47.166 --> 00:17:48.586
So doing that, getting some of

00:17:48.586 --> 00:17:52.606
that kind of authentic
success going in is important.

00:17:52.606 --> 00:17:55.406
And then as we think about how to,

00:17:55.406 --> 00:17:58.246
one of the things that
research talks about a lot

00:17:58.246 --> 00:18:01.286
is a difference between what
they call external motivation

00:18:01.286 --> 00:18:03.006
and intrinsic motivation.

00:18:03.006 --> 00:18:05.906
And so external motivation
is being motivated by things

00:18:05.906 --> 00:18:07.326
from the outside.

00:18:07.326 --> 00:18:10.366
So like getting rewards
for what you're doing

00:18:10.366 --> 00:18:12.136
and intrinsic is being motivated

00:18:12.136 --> 00:18:14.406
from internal things
because it makes you happy,

00:18:14.406 --> 00:18:17.346
it brings you joy, you
find it interesting.

00:18:17.346 --> 00:18:20.116
And so one of the questions
is how to move kids

00:18:20.116 --> 00:18:22.166
from not wanting to do something

00:18:22.166 --> 00:18:25.116
to maybe being internally
motivated to do it.

00:18:25.116 --> 00:18:28.046
And so I get also a lot of questions about

00:18:28.046 --> 00:18:30.336
is giving my kids rewards
just bribing them,

00:18:30.336 --> 00:18:31.886
that seems wrong.

00:18:31.886 --> 00:18:35.076
But it's okay to use
rewards sometimes to bridge

00:18:35.076 --> 00:18:37.996
the gap between, I don't wanna do this

00:18:37.996 --> 00:18:41.876
and to get a little taste of it.

00:18:41.876 --> 00:18:43.616
That getting a little success

00:18:43.616 --> 00:18:45.226
that will lead them to success.

00:18:45.226 --> 00:18:48.946
So it's not bad to think
about small rewards

00:18:48.946 --> 00:18:51.626
and talking about maybe
it's playing a board game,

00:18:51.626 --> 00:18:53.846
maybe it's picking what's for dinner.

00:18:53.846 --> 00:18:57.696
Those kinds of things is
rewards that something

00:18:57.696 --> 00:19:00.786
that's meaningful for
your student, your kid

00:19:00.786 --> 00:19:04.426
that gives them a little
reason to try something

00:19:04.426 --> 00:19:06.346
and then give them experience of it

00:19:06.346 --> 00:19:07.816
that they can then build on.

00:19:07.816 --> 00:19:09.256
So that's one of the ways we can think

00:19:09.256 --> 00:19:11.086
about kind of moving towards being

00:19:11.086 --> 00:19:12.929
more intrinsically motivated.

00:19:14.549 --> 00:19:16.366
- I like that and it resonates with me

00:19:16.366 --> 00:19:18.556
and it makes me feel
less guilty last night

00:19:18.556 --> 00:19:22.156
for bribing my kids with
being able to watch Shira

00:19:22.156 --> 00:19:24.216
so that they can take a bath.

00:19:24.216 --> 00:19:25.606
But I would like to believe

00:19:25.606 --> 00:19:27.786
that extrinsic motivation over time,

00:19:27.786 --> 00:19:30.956
they will recognize the
intrinsic benefits of being clean

00:19:31.964 --> 00:19:33.698
and not stinky.

00:19:33.698 --> 00:19:36.446
- You've also got society helping you

00:19:36.446 --> 00:19:37.503
with that a little bit once

00:19:37.503 --> 00:19:39.273
they start getting more of that.

00:19:39.273 --> 00:19:40.106
(laughing)

00:19:40.106 --> 00:19:41.596
- That might be extrinsic motivation too.

00:19:41.596 --> 00:19:43.116
When you're stinky,

00:19:43.116 --> 00:19:45.270
people might give you
some external feedback

00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:46.926
that makes you realize

00:19:46.926 --> 00:19:48.671
that you need to take a bath.

00:19:48.671 --> 00:19:51.206
More questions here.

00:19:51.206 --> 00:19:52.316
So how do you,

00:19:52.316 --> 00:19:54.966
this is from Facebook
Andrea Houk Lara says,

00:19:54.966 --> 00:19:57.766
how do you motivate preschoolers
in a virtual classroom?

00:20:00.096 --> 00:20:02.396
- There's one of the things to think about

00:20:02.396 --> 00:20:04.126
with kids who are really bad age

00:20:04.126 --> 00:20:05.356
is not even motivation,

00:20:05.356 --> 00:20:07.696
but just attention spans

00:20:07.696 --> 00:20:10.186
and making sure first that you

00:20:10.186 --> 00:20:12.126
have realistic expectations

00:20:12.126 --> 00:20:15.996
of what a preschooler
can do in a zoom class.

00:20:15.996 --> 00:20:20.126
We're talking fairly shorts timeframes,

00:20:20.126 --> 00:20:22.676
a few minutes on any particular activity.

00:20:22.676 --> 00:20:25.536
If you look to things like Sesame Streets,

00:20:25.536 --> 00:20:29.686
in terms of how they're
switching to a new thing

00:20:29.686 --> 00:20:31.576
that they're doing.

00:20:31.576 --> 00:20:34.616
We can't expect, we
shouldn't expect preschoolers

00:20:34.616 --> 00:20:37.283
to be able to sit for
a 30 minute zoom class

00:20:37.283 --> 00:20:41.066
doing all of the same kind of activity.

00:20:41.066 --> 00:20:43.346
So one, you're gonna wanna
talk to your child's teacher,

00:20:43.346 --> 00:20:45.729
make sure that they're doing,

00:20:47.056 --> 00:20:50.246
making those switches to
doing different things,

00:20:50.246 --> 00:20:52.936
thinking about how you're
structured in that time

00:20:52.936 --> 00:20:54.656
that they're expected to be in.

00:20:54.656 --> 00:20:57.316
So that's the first thing I would say.

00:20:57.316 --> 00:21:01.066
And so then the second
piece is just thinking about

00:21:01.066 --> 00:21:03.846
what is it that is in those activities

00:21:03.846 --> 00:21:06.126
that is related to things
that they're excited about,

00:21:06.126 --> 00:21:07.446
things that they're interested in,

00:21:07.446 --> 00:21:08.966
make those connections.

00:21:08.966 --> 00:21:13.966
Hey, did you ever wonder why are our dog

00:21:14.236 --> 00:21:18.426
and our cats one goes
out for a walk every day

00:21:18.426 --> 00:21:20.096
and the cat doesn't, I don't know.

00:21:20.096 --> 00:21:21.946
Just like silly questions

00:21:21.946 --> 00:21:24.596
that you might be able to think about

00:21:24.596 --> 00:21:27.276
and think about how, what they're doing in

00:21:27.276 --> 00:21:30.216
the classroom can bring
those kinds of ideas in.

00:21:30.216 --> 00:21:32.326
But with younger kids again,

00:21:32.326 --> 00:21:33.866
manage your expectations

00:21:33.866 --> 00:21:34.816
about what they're gonna be able

00:21:34.816 --> 00:21:36.499
to do in this environment.

00:21:37.516 --> 00:21:38.586
- Yeah, now it makes a lot of sense.

00:21:38.586 --> 00:21:39.746
And I would just add to that

00:21:39.746 --> 00:21:42.326
I have my youngest is almost six,

00:21:42.326 --> 00:21:43.356
five and a half years old

00:21:43.356 --> 00:21:45.616
and what I've seen his
teachers do really well

00:21:45.616 --> 00:21:48.156
is just to quirky funny
things all the time,

00:21:48.156 --> 00:21:49.916
intersperse whatever they're trying to do

00:21:49.916 --> 00:21:52.836
with just some goofiness,
some moving around

00:21:52.836 --> 00:21:53.926
and to your point Kristen,

00:21:53.926 --> 00:21:55.986
like not do long stretches

00:21:55.986 --> 00:21:57.086
and four or five or six year old,

00:21:57.086 --> 00:21:59.656
a long stretch could even
be 15 minutes or 20 minutes.

00:21:59.656 --> 00:22:01.166
But try to break it up and try to make

00:22:01.166 --> 00:22:02.786
it as interactive as possible.

00:22:02.786 --> 00:22:05.966
I also do wanna make sure
folks tune in for tomorrow

00:22:05.966 --> 00:22:07.136
as there gonna be a 100th show

00:22:07.136 --> 00:22:08.466
and the person who can help answer

00:22:08.466 --> 00:22:11.266
some of these questions about
motivating kindergartners

00:22:11.266 --> 00:22:14.186
and preschoolers, Tabatha Rosproy.

00:22:14.186 --> 00:22:17.136
She's a 2020 National Teacher of the Year.

00:22:17.136 --> 00:22:19.026
So hopefully she can give us some tips

00:22:19.026 --> 00:22:21.876
as well on motivating that crowd

00:22:21.876 --> 00:22:24.606
which I'm sure I can
benefit from here at home.

00:22:24.606 --> 00:22:26.296
So, from YouTube I have a question...

00:22:26.296 --> 00:22:28.886
- I would also say just to echo that,

00:22:28.886 --> 00:22:32.566
that teachers have great
experience doing this

00:22:32.566 --> 00:22:33.816
and the lots of good tips.

00:22:33.816 --> 00:22:36.559
So, just echo encouraging
people to tune in for that.

00:22:38.016 --> 00:22:40.856
- Yeah, my other last
tip and I would even call

00:22:40.856 --> 00:22:42.196
this a pro tip 'cause I'm not a pro

00:22:42.196 --> 00:22:44.198
by any stretch of
imagination is stick with

00:22:44.198 --> 00:22:46.476
the young kids even they are very capable

00:22:46.476 --> 00:22:48.536
of eventually forming
some of these habits.

00:22:48.536 --> 00:22:50.956
And once they see some wins,

00:22:50.956 --> 00:22:52.766
they too can get a little
bit more motivated.

00:22:52.766 --> 00:22:55.446
Well, I've seen that with
my own with my own son

00:22:55.446 --> 00:22:58.986
who's five since distance
learning started.

00:22:58.986 --> 00:23:01.846
So from YouTube Kaushik Jha says

00:23:01.846 --> 00:23:03.086
I started feeling depressed

00:23:03.086 --> 00:23:04.956
and thinking about goals, COVID

00:23:04.956 --> 00:23:06.536
literally makes me go through anxiety.

00:23:06.536 --> 00:23:08.606
So what advice do you have for that Kris?

00:23:08.606 --> 00:23:10.256
I don't think Kaushik is alone.

00:23:10.256 --> 00:23:11.976
I think there's a lot of folks

00:23:11.976 --> 00:23:14.646
that you have COVID, you have the economy,

00:23:14.646 --> 00:23:16.816
you have other stuff going on in the world

00:23:16.816 --> 00:23:17.756
that is causing a lot of stress

00:23:17.756 --> 00:23:21.116
and then on top of that,
people are trying to figure out

00:23:21.116 --> 00:23:23.486
their own life and I don't know if Kaushik

00:23:23.486 --> 00:23:25.866
is a student or is working or whatever

00:23:25.866 --> 00:23:27.136
but what are your goals?

00:23:27.136 --> 00:23:28.356
I'm I achieving what I need to?

00:23:28.356 --> 00:23:29.716
Is it easier or harder now

00:23:29.716 --> 00:23:31.206
I'm less connected with friends.

00:23:31.206 --> 00:23:33.726
How do you recommend people navigate this

00:23:33.726 --> 00:23:36.180
and maybe try to try to get out of that

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:40.326
and try to maybe hopefully
make some forward progress

00:23:40.326 --> 00:23:41.476
on some of their goals.

00:23:42.766 --> 00:23:45.636
- Absolutely, so I'm gonna take this from

00:23:45.636 --> 00:23:48.606
first starting just from
the learning perspective

00:23:48.606 --> 00:23:52.466
which and just say, keep it simple.

00:23:52.466 --> 00:23:55.316
We don't have to do everything

00:23:55.316 --> 00:23:58.586
while we're in this COVID situation.

00:23:58.586 --> 00:24:00.036
We'll be out of it at some point.

00:24:00.036 --> 00:24:01.266
We'll get a vaccine,

00:24:01.266 --> 00:24:03.426
we'll be able to go out in the world.

00:24:03.426 --> 00:24:05.436
If we can just make sure we're keeping up

00:24:05.436 --> 00:24:08.606
the foundations right now,
that's the important thing.

00:24:08.606 --> 00:24:11.086
So for younger kids, just make sure

00:24:11.086 --> 00:24:15.556
they're doing some math,
they're doing some reading,

00:24:15.556 --> 00:24:18.686
they're getting some
keeping up their curiosity

00:24:18.686 --> 00:24:20.106
about the world.

00:24:20.106 --> 00:24:22.576
Those are really the important
things that you're doing.

00:24:22.576 --> 00:24:25.146
For students that are a
little bit older again,

00:24:25.146 --> 00:24:27.906
think about what are the
most important things

00:24:27.906 --> 00:24:29.696
for them to be learning this year.

00:24:29.696 --> 00:24:31.526
What are the biggest pieces

00:24:31.526 --> 00:24:33.756
that we wanna make sure they get?

00:24:33.756 --> 00:24:38.756
And let yourself go with all of the things

00:24:38.786 --> 00:24:40.906
that come up in terms of
learning on top of that.

00:24:40.906 --> 00:24:43.266
It's okay if you don't get them now.

00:24:43.266 --> 00:24:45.346
We're all really resilient

00:24:45.346 --> 00:24:47.966
and we'll be able to pick up again when

00:24:47.966 --> 00:24:51.226
this is all off and we
can get back together

00:24:51.226 --> 00:24:54.712
and do in person, learning and focus again

00:24:54.712 --> 00:24:56.276
on all of these things.

00:24:56.276 --> 00:24:57.886
So that's my first thing is,

00:24:57.886 --> 00:24:59.956
it's okay if you're not doing everything

00:24:59.956 --> 00:25:02.649
that you were doing in the before times.

00:25:03.706 --> 00:25:06.646
So it's okay when you set goals,

00:25:06.646 --> 00:25:08.106
we talked about progress monitoring there,

00:25:08.106 --> 00:25:09.336
make sure you're on track,

00:25:09.336 --> 00:25:11.326
but another skill is actually realizing

00:25:11.326 --> 00:25:13.336
when this isn't gonna work.

00:25:13.336 --> 00:25:14.886
I can't do this right now.

00:25:14.886 --> 00:25:17.536
In modifying what your goals are

00:25:17.536 --> 00:25:19.686
and thinking about whether maybe

00:25:19.686 --> 00:25:21.616
it's you're gonna extend

00:25:21.616 --> 00:25:23.416
the time it takes to meet that goal.

00:25:23.416 --> 00:25:24.516
Maybe you're gonna change what

00:25:24.516 --> 00:25:26.006
the goal is a little bit.

00:25:26.006 --> 00:25:27.956
That's okay, our goals don't have,

00:25:27.956 --> 00:25:29.496
we're not like carved in stone.

00:25:29.496 --> 00:25:30.836
We can change them.

00:25:30.836 --> 00:25:32.776
And it's actually quite a skill

00:25:32.776 --> 00:25:35.466
to be able to recognize
when you're off track

00:25:35.466 --> 00:25:39.756
and be able to find ways
to either get on track

00:25:39.756 --> 00:25:41.976
or to modify where what your past is

00:25:41.976 --> 00:25:43.736
and where you think you're gonna go.

00:25:43.736 --> 00:25:44.766
We say one more thing,

00:25:44.766 --> 00:25:48.606
I also have a background
as a School Psychologist.

00:25:48.606 --> 00:25:50.516
And if you feel like the anxiety

00:25:50.516 --> 00:25:54.886
that you're experiencing is
interfering with your work

00:25:54.886 --> 00:25:57.116
in your life and that worry

00:25:57.116 --> 00:25:59.156
the thoughts you're having
are actually interfering

00:25:59.156 --> 00:26:02.746
with kind of the daily
things you need to do,

00:26:02.746 --> 00:26:05.236
don't be afraid to seek
help for that either.

00:26:05.236 --> 00:26:07.366
If you've got kids, you can
talk to their pediatrician,

00:26:07.366 --> 00:26:10.336
you can talk to your general
practitioner for some advice

00:26:10.336 --> 00:26:12.076
and help as well but make sure

00:26:12.076 --> 00:26:13.476
that you're staying healthy.

00:26:14.866 --> 00:26:17.426
- Yeah, I'll just echo that
last point Kristen made.

00:26:17.426 --> 00:26:20.066
Sometimes you're like a
frog in boiling water.

00:26:20.066 --> 00:26:21.836
You don't realize that
things are getting bad

00:26:21.836 --> 00:26:23.076
until they maybe get too bad.

00:26:23.076 --> 00:26:25.306
And so very as soon as you get a sense,

00:26:25.306 --> 00:26:27.476
it never hurts to go talk to folks,

00:26:27.476 --> 00:26:29.626
get help, et cetera, et cetera.

00:26:29.626 --> 00:26:31.946
The other thing I would throw out is,

00:26:31.946 --> 00:26:34.176
sometimes if your goals are too ambitious,

00:26:34.176 --> 00:26:35.366
that can become debilitating

00:26:35.366 --> 00:26:36.626
because they feel daunting

00:26:36.626 --> 00:26:37.793
or they feel very hard to start on.

00:26:37.793 --> 00:26:39.836
And I think one thing that
I've heard from Kristen,

00:26:39.836 --> 00:26:41.696
not just over the last several minutes

00:26:41.696 --> 00:26:45.036
but in the past is just of your goals

00:26:45.036 --> 00:26:47.286
need to be things that
you can get some wins on

00:26:47.286 --> 00:26:49.115
so that you can kind of
notch them in your belt

00:26:49.115 --> 00:26:51.456
and move on from that.

00:26:51.456 --> 00:26:52.876
The other thing I'd recommend is,

00:26:52.876 --> 00:26:54.096
and I've talked about this before

00:26:54.096 --> 00:26:55.696
is try out meditation.

00:26:55.696 --> 00:26:58.526
Meditation it isn't some weird thing.

00:26:58.526 --> 00:27:00.486
All it is, is sitting quietly

00:27:00.486 --> 00:27:02.506
and becoming observant of your thoughts

00:27:02.506 --> 00:27:04.516
and then letting your
mind still a little bit.

00:27:04.516 --> 00:27:07.166
And you'll realize that
a lot of those anxieties,

00:27:07.166 --> 00:27:09.386
a lot of those maybe
even negative feelings,

00:27:09.386 --> 00:27:11.096
they come from a part of your brain,

00:27:11.096 --> 00:27:13.246
but that isn't your entire self.

00:27:13.246 --> 00:27:14.566
I wouldn't call that a substitute

00:27:14.566 --> 00:27:16.546
for getting help if you need it.

00:27:16.546 --> 00:27:18.066
But look into it even if you're able

00:27:18.066 --> 00:27:20.006
to do two, three minutes a day,

00:27:20.006 --> 00:27:21.216
I've talked about before.

00:27:21.216 --> 00:27:23.446
I used to get anxious on planes.

00:27:23.446 --> 00:27:24.896
I still go do a little bit.

00:27:24.896 --> 00:27:27.406
But by meditating, it
really helped me actually

00:27:27.406 --> 00:27:29.196
not only not get anxious on planes,

00:27:29.196 --> 00:27:30.676
but in a lot of other circumstances

00:27:30.676 --> 00:27:33.946
where I might have otherwise
gotten a little bit worked up.

00:27:33.946 --> 00:27:34.926
So we have time maybe

00:27:34.926 --> 00:27:36.366
for one more question

00:27:36.366 --> 00:27:38.946
from Facebook Rohonie Arjune is at saying,

00:27:38.946 --> 00:27:40.436
my child although very chatty

00:27:40.436 --> 00:27:41.446
and extroverted at home with

00:27:41.446 --> 00:27:42.406
and with the family,

00:27:42.406 --> 00:27:45.276
he's extremely shy with
speaking up on video

00:27:45.276 --> 00:27:46.336
with his teacher in class.

00:27:46.336 --> 00:27:48.176
How can I motivate his participation?

00:27:48.176 --> 00:27:50.476
I've actually heard this
a lot, Kristen ideas.

00:27:51.476 --> 00:27:54.186
- Yeah, so there's a
couple of things to do.

00:27:54.186 --> 00:27:56.816
So one, is make sure that you know

00:27:56.816 --> 00:28:00.946
what is the kind of
importance of speaking out.

00:28:00.946 --> 00:28:03.106
So sometimes it's not that important

00:28:03.106 --> 00:28:05.396
if they're not sharing all the time

00:28:05.396 --> 00:28:06.886
and they're a little bit more quiet

00:28:06.886 --> 00:28:08.236
and that can be okay.

00:28:08.236 --> 00:28:11.386
But one of the things that
speaking out actually does

00:28:11.386 --> 00:28:13.586
is it helps us work through

00:28:13.586 --> 00:28:15.296
the ideas that we're learning.

00:28:15.296 --> 00:28:17.766
If you explain them in your own words,

00:28:17.766 --> 00:28:19.986
back to the teacher or
you're answering a question

00:28:19.986 --> 00:28:22.286
for the teacher, what that's
doing is you're putting

00:28:22.286 --> 00:28:24.526
into your own words the
things you're learning

00:28:24.526 --> 00:28:26.026
and you give the teacher the opportunity

00:28:26.026 --> 00:28:28.526
to maybe correct what's going on there.

00:28:28.526 --> 00:28:32.106
So if you understand, if
that's one of the purposes,

00:28:32.106 --> 00:28:34.666
you wanna figure out how
to make sure your child's

00:28:34.666 --> 00:28:35.696
getting that experience.

00:28:35.696 --> 00:28:37.666
So it might be actually talking

00:28:37.666 --> 00:28:39.016
to you a little bit about what are

00:28:39.016 --> 00:28:41.546
the things that they are learning,

00:28:41.546 --> 00:28:43.226
what are the things, how
would they have answered

00:28:43.226 --> 00:28:44.986
that question that maybe
they didn't speak up

00:28:44.986 --> 00:28:47.566
to answer so that you can work with them

00:28:47.566 --> 00:28:48.726
to do that too

00:28:49.936 --> 00:28:53.606
Second, make sure they
know like the protocols,

00:28:53.606 --> 00:28:55.066
what are the norms in their classroom

00:28:55.066 --> 00:28:55.946
for speaking up?

00:28:55.946 --> 00:28:59.456
I know I get on calls
where there's 10 people,

00:28:59.456 --> 00:29:01.266
just 10 people online,

00:29:01.266 --> 00:29:03.546
and it's really hard to like get in,

00:29:03.546 --> 00:29:06.816
like to get heard and then it's your turn.

00:29:06.816 --> 00:29:10.126
And so find out how do they,

00:29:10.126 --> 00:29:12.476
is there a virtual hand
raising function in

00:29:12.476 --> 00:29:13.686
the platform you use?

00:29:13.686 --> 00:29:15.496
Is there a way that they can,

00:29:15.496 --> 00:29:16.436
how do they indicate when they

00:29:16.436 --> 00:29:18.016
do have something to say

00:29:18.016 --> 00:29:19.766
that they want to say something

00:29:19.766 --> 00:29:21.266
and make sure they know how to that,

00:29:21.266 --> 00:29:23.666
that's the next piece.

00:29:23.666 --> 00:29:25.476
And then finally think about the,

00:29:25.476 --> 00:29:27.866
in the same ways that we
talked about motivating

00:29:27.866 --> 00:29:29.386
to do anything.

00:29:29.386 --> 00:29:33.146
How can we think about setting some goals

00:29:33.146 --> 00:29:34.416
around how many times,

00:29:34.416 --> 00:29:37.856
hey, let's see if you
can speak up two times

00:29:37.856 --> 00:29:40.166
in this next hour long class

00:29:40.166 --> 00:29:42.066
and contribute to the discussion

00:29:42.066 --> 00:29:43.946
and say where those are.

00:29:43.946 --> 00:29:48.226
So those kinds of think
about those same principles.

00:29:48.226 --> 00:29:49.466
If they're not doing it,

00:29:49.466 --> 00:29:51.616
do you wanna bring in
some external rewards

00:29:51.616 --> 00:29:53.409
for them when they do do that?

00:29:54.576 --> 00:29:57.276
And finally just make
sure you've always got

00:29:57.276 --> 00:29:59.716
that connection to your child's teacher

00:29:59.716 --> 00:30:01.686
so that your child's teacher's aware

00:30:01.686 --> 00:30:03.496
that maybe they're having
some of these worries

00:30:03.496 --> 00:30:04.466
and some of these concerns

00:30:04.466 --> 00:30:06.352
about how to speak up as well.

00:30:06.352 --> 00:30:11.352
So those are some individual
thoughts there about

00:30:11.666 --> 00:30:14.306
how to get kind of kids
going in these zoom calls.

00:30:14.306 --> 00:30:16.419
We've probably all
experienced it ourselves.

00:30:18.023 --> 00:30:19.256
- No, absolutely I think I've been on

00:30:19.256 --> 00:30:20.206
some of those calls

00:30:20.206 --> 00:30:22.356
with Kristen where have
trouble getting in.

00:30:22.356 --> 00:30:24.336
I hope it wasn't my fault.

00:30:24.336 --> 00:30:26.036
Well, Kristen thank you so much.

00:30:26.036 --> 00:30:26.966
I hope we do this again.

00:30:26.966 --> 00:30:28.356
As you can see, a lot of folks have

00:30:28.356 --> 00:30:31.546
a lot of questions about
engagement and motivation.

00:30:31.546 --> 00:30:33.476
So I don't think this is
the end of our conversation,

00:30:33.476 --> 00:30:35.216
but thanks so much for it...

00:30:35.216 --> 00:30:36.049
- let me just say,

00:30:36.049 --> 00:30:37.856
actually if you have more questions,

00:30:37.856 --> 00:30:41.266
we're doing a Webinar next week

00:30:41.266 --> 00:30:42.246
and you can sign up.

00:30:42.246 --> 00:30:44.146
It's the place you see here.

00:30:44.146 --> 00:30:47.406
So one more plug before
we go, September 30th,

00:30:47.406 --> 00:30:49.919
four Pacific, seven Eastern.

00:30:52.086 --> 00:30:53.326
- Awesome.

00:30:53.326 --> 00:30:54.159
Thanks Kristen.

00:30:54.159 --> 00:30:55.776
Yeah, definitely join that Webinar

00:30:55.776 --> 00:30:58.126
a lot more good information
like you just got.

00:30:58.126 --> 00:30:59.456
Also wanna remind everyone

00:30:59.456 --> 00:31:01.356
for tomorrow's Live stream

00:31:01.356 --> 00:31:04.246
on National 2020 Teacher of the Years

00:31:04.246 --> 00:31:06.183
as I mentioned Tabitha Rosproy

00:31:06.183 --> 00:31:08.536
and we're gonna be able to talk about,

00:31:08.536 --> 00:31:10.276
well she's national teacher of the year,

00:31:10.276 --> 00:31:11.816
but especially working with younger kids.

00:31:11.816 --> 00:31:13.156
So I think we'll be able to continue some

00:31:13.156 --> 00:31:14.216
of these questions we've
been talking about,

00:31:14.216 --> 00:31:16.546
about motivation,
especially with younger kids

00:31:16.546 --> 00:31:17.379
and distance learning.

00:31:17.379 --> 00:31:19.846
So look forward to
seeing you all tomorrow.

00:31:19.846 --> 00:31:20.959
Have a good day.

